WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.310
All right, Katie.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:11.310
Last year, I'm in an Uber
on the way home from work.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.230
As one does.
As one does.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:15.569
The Uber driver has got
his crypto account open.

00:00:15.569 --> 00:00:16.667
While he's driving.
While he's driving,

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:19.560
and he is showing me all
the money that he's made

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.570
on crypto over the last few months.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.070 position:66%
And I'm not talking Bitcoin or Ether here,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.150
I'm talking coins.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:24.983
You can say that.

00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:25.890
I just did.
Okay.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.220
And he's like, okay, when
it starts, it's so easy.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.110
When it starts going up, you buy,

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.730
and then when it gets
really high, you sell.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.690
I've heard that before.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:34.920
Well, that's what he was doing.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:35.753
Yeah.

00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:37.920 position:61%
And all I could think of to myself was,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.420
this is the top, you know?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.370
When your Uber driver's
showing you how much money

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.920
he's made in crypto and
how you gotta get in on it.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.800
And I'm thinking, this is not
gonna end well for this guy.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.117
And sure enough, here we
are a few months later.

00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.770
$2 trillion in crypto
value has been wiped out.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.780
And I'm not hearing Uber drivers

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.670
talk about their crypto anymore.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:58.950
Well, if that story's true,
and it sounds super fake,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.183
let's just establish that.
It's true.

00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:02.250
I promise it happened.
Okay, well, if it's true,

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.740 position:63%
it actually was pretty close to the top.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.380
If you think about where
we were around November

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.350
of last year, close to a
record high on Bitcoin.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.350
And to your point, we've just
seen this tremendous draw down

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.187
of wealth since then.

00:01:14.187 --> 00:01:15.780
And I actually think that creates

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.450
one of the more interesting
moments in crypto.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.250 position:59%
After the summer we just lived through

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.560 position:59%
where it felt like everything blow up,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.930
now's the moment where the industry

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.930
sort of has to decide
where to go from here.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.620
Whether this is a 2018 moment
after that boom and bust,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.290
which went on to build
an even bigger bubble,

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.770
or whether we're entering a
phase of the crypto ecosystem

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.080
where it's more mature.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.460
So luckily for us, we have eight
episodes to try to do that.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.950 position:61%
I have all these papers in front of me,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.190
and we're gonna try to
figure out what moment

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:51.450
we're actually in right now.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.810
And funnily enough, we
actually prepared something.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.373
No kidding?
Yeah, we did.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:03.000
It only took a few months,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.110
but more than $2 trillion in
crypto wealth was wiped out.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.940
We're talking wreckage
that was far and wide here.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.770 position:66%
Yeah, we got the billionaires on one side,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:14.910
but we got everyday people
who lost their life savings.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.010
We saw currencies collapse.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.570 position:58%
There were mass layoffs at companies,

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.040
and some of these
companies were once valued

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.740
at billions of dollars
declared bankruptcy.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.573
So what happened?

00:02:23.573 --> 00:02:24.750
Well, there was the collapse

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.760 position:66%
of this so-called algorithmic stable coin.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.700
And what that did is it
set off this chain reaction

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.130
that brought one of the best
known crypto hedge funds

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:33.270
to its knees.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.280 position:58%
We gotta go back to May to understand

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.810
what really happened here.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.460
The collapse of Terra UST.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.230
This is an algorithmic stable coin,

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.180
and it once topped the charts.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.090 position:63%
Now, it's not exactly clear what set off

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.860
the slump and demand for UST,

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.350
but then we saw this death spiral.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.370
And it knocked UST off its $1 peg.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.770
Think about this as like
the crypto equivalent

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:57.270
of a bank run.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.940
At the same time, crypto
had been falling in price

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.100
since the end of last year.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.930
But then as Terra was melting down,

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.850
the panic started to spread.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:09.390
And it dropped the price
of Bitcoin by nearly 30%

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:10.890
in just a week.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.380
The problem is that
lenders were using Bitcoin

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.690
for collateral and that
just led to carnage

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.280
across the industry.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.750
Take Three Arrows Capital.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.240
It was this previously
invincible hedge fund.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.550 position:61%
It once managed as much as $10 billion,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.290
and it declared bankruptcy.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.310
This was just a mess,

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.260
but it's a mess that
we've gotta make sense of

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.630
and we've gotta understand.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.130 position:59%
A $60 billion collapse of an ecosystem

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.800
that led to a massive decline
in the price of Bitcoin.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.230
And then the outright
collapse of companies.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.760
It was contagion,

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.910
but it was contagion that raises
really important questions.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.860
What's the collateral
damage to an industry

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.360
after an event like this?

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.580
And perhaps most importantly,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.683 position:59%
have people just lost faith in crypto?

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.070 position:59%
All right, we have a lot of questions.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.020
We've got Stacy Marie Ishmael.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.450 position:63%
She leads crypto coverage for Bloomberg,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.180 position:63%
arguably the busiest woman at Bloomberg.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:04.940
It's been a moment.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.750
It's been a moment.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:11.700
And I guess when I think
back to the summer of 2021,

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.905
that run up to last November

00:04:13.905 --> 00:04:16.260
where we reached the
record high in Bitcoin,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.670
one of the big bullish mantras
was institutional adoptions,

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.970
sort of traditional Wall
Street embracing crypto.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.860
And when you say that
we're kind of learning

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:30.420
the lessons of 2008, what is
the reputational damage here?

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.303
Is that no longer really
a narrative that holds?

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:39.030
It's been interesting to
see institutions plunge in,

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.070
not just like a toe in the water,

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.020 position:59%
at the same time that retail investors

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.310
are a little bit more skittish

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.860
and a little bit more inclined
to sit on the sidelines.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.690 position:64%
So you know, just in the past six months,

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.110
you've had BlackRock announce
a partnership with Coinbase,

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:54.870
you've had Goldman Sachs make
certain types of derivatives

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.970
available to clients.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:00.420
Even more recently, you have
kind of Citadel, Fidelity,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.460
and others saying they're
gonna back something

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.060 position:58%
like a crypto esque exchange program.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.900
So I do think that one of
the interesting features

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.580
of this institutional
adoption is they're like,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:12.930
we're already regulated.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.830
We know how to do this.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.660 position:59%
We have all of the available licenses.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.520 position:58%
We understand how to man manage risk,

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.020
and they're seeing opportunities.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:24.020
Is there concern that right
now the everyday person,

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.710
the normal retail person,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.960
is just completely scared away

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.930
and is going to potentially
miss an opportunity

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:35.930
because they got worried about
what happened post Matt Damon

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.410
and they're just saying,

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.360 position:61%
I'm not gonna touch this stuff anymore.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.040
There's a few different ways

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.180
that we're thinking about that.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:46.620 position:63%
On the one hand, you know, if you were a

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.720
to the moon, yield farming,

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.450
I'm looking at 19 different
protocols, you know,

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.940
you're like, yeah, down
with decentralization.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.010
On the other hand, if
you're somebody who watched

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:58.140
perhaps your friends, perhaps
your family, perhaps yourself,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.190
lose your life savings in
something like Voyager or Celsius,

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:04.200 position:64%
then maybe you're gonna look at a Citadel

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:05.850 position:64%
or a Fidelity or a BlackRock and be like,

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.250
they seem like they
know what they're doing.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.170 position:66%
Their presence might be reassuring to you.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:13.650 position:63%
So you've got sort of competing dynamics

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.090
in the market at the same time.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.220
Sort of the adults in the room.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.330 position:61%
That's what they would like you to say.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:21.330
Yeah, here I am saying
it, so there you go.

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:22.380
I am curious though,

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:24.810 position:63%
I mean it feels like a lot of the chaos,

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.710
the contagion was centered
around these crypto lenders.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.710
And I'd love if you could
bring out your crystal ball

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.630
and gaze into it and tell us,

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:38.010
are we ever gonna go back to
the days of like 18% returns

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.953 position:56%
or lending returns, or is that over?

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:44.040 position:64%
If I can answer this question accurately,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.060
I'd be in a very different job.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:47.700 position:56%
However, I will say that as a person

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:50.160
who covered the financial crisis,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.590
for a while they were like,
we'll never go back to X, Y, Z.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:53.610
And then like three years later,

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.860
somebody was like, I've got
this really exotic product

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:57.180
I wanna sell you.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.610
And the memories and the timelines

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.140 position:56%
that we tend to operate on in crypto

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.820
are even shorter, right?

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:07.440 position:64%
Where sort of in the dying embers of Luna

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.230
and the associated stable coin,

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.460
you had other folks being like,

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.820
this is why our algorithmic
stable coin is gonna be fine.

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.060
So I think there is an
an kind of an inertia

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:22.470
built into the system of
anything that we invent

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:26.250
can only be positive unless
there's widespread evidence

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.890
to the contrary, and
then we'll just do V2.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.410
Okay, so for the past few years,

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:33.060 position:63%
people in the space have been telling me

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.890
when I don't understand something,

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.990
it's like, it's the early
days of the internet.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.660 position:59%
This is like the 1990s when, you know,

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:42.390 position:59%
Andreessen had just invented Netscape.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.550 position:63%
And you know, we're trying to figure out

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.560
what you can do with this thing.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.460
If that's accurate, did what we see

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.970 position:64%
in the early part of 2022, the explosion,

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.683
was that the.com bust?

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.340 position:56%
One of the things about the.com bust

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.790
is there were clear winners
that emerged from it,

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.590
but you couldn't really
tell who those winners would

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:05.970
be at the time.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:10.230
And so I do think that
some of the immediate

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:13.470
speculative excess got
wrong out of the market

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:15.390 position:66%
very hard and very fast and very painfully

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.070
for a lot of people involved.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:20.340 position:59%
But you know, it took years for Amazon

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.400
to become Amazon and it took
a while for people to be like,

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.410
ah, pets.com is pets.com.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:29.580
And we are still in sort of
the immediate aftermath of that

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.500
where it's like you
don't know who the Amazon

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:33.300 position:56%
is gonna be and who the pets.com is.

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.740 position:66%
Right, I mean, Amazon's actually only been

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:37.950
a profitable company reliably
for not that many years.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:38.850
Exactly.

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:41.700
So there was this really
grim time a few months ago

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.260
when things were crashing.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.360 position:61%
Message boards were filled with people,

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:47.010 position:63%
anecdotes of people who lost everything.

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.200
Our colleagues here at
Bloomberg published a story

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.690
about people who lost
everything with Voyager.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:53.520 position:66%
I mean, there were people who were talking

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.670
about self-harm in these forums.

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.890
And I'm just wondering what
we've heard from the industry

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.450
over that period of time.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.720
I wouldn't say there's been
a ton of self-flagellation

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:08.310 position:66%
or even kind of explicit, whoops, our bad.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.538
It's much more around, well, one,

00:09:11.538 --> 00:09:13.410
a lot of people are getting sued.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.680
And when you're being sued
actively by thousands of people,

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:19.320
it is perhaps against
the advice of counsel

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:20.940
to be like, well, we made mistakes

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.520
because that would be appearing
to admit guilt of some kind.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:24.420
Makes sense.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.980
But I also think there's a
nihilistic streak in crypto

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.920
that suggests that, well,
you should have known

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.240
what you were getting into.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.453 position:59%
Like, how come you invested more money

00:09:36.453 --> 00:09:37.530
than you could afford?

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:38.363
That's on you.

00:09:38.363 --> 00:09:40.590
If you were margin and
leveraged up to your eyeballs,

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:41.423
that's on you.

00:09:41.423 --> 00:09:43.920
We didn't tell you to put
your life savings in crypto.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:45.720
And I do think that's a
problem for the industry

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.740
because it's the kind of
rhetoric that regulators

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:52.710
do not seem best pleased by

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:56.730
because it reminds them of
things like the earlier days

00:09:56.730 --> 00:09:58.410
of sports betting and gambling

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.900 position:59%
before those were recognized as having

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.520
potentially addictive properties

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.710
and the messaging around
that really had to change.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.670
So I do think there has
been an insufficient

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:11.880
amount of contrition
relative to the amount

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.730
of boosting that there was
by a lot of these folks.

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:17.700
What's happened instead is
people are just deleting tweets,

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:19.770 position:61%
taking down YouTube videos, being like,

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:21.990
haha just kidding, JK.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.420 position:66%
And I don't think that that is necessarily

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.970
endearing themselves to the
people who did lose everything.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:29.523
But again, there's always
a new buyer, right?

00:10:30.957 --> 00:10:33.207
There's always somebody who's
coming in for the first time.

00:10:33.207 --> 00:10:34.893
And I worry about that new buyer.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.670
With us now, Meltem Demirors,
extremely cool woman.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:45.330
Also the chief strategy
officer of CoinShares.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:48.420
Meltem, when we think
about the past few months,

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:50.580
really even the past few
years we extended out,

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.550
people, cranks have been warning

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:54.810
that this would end in tears.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:57.900
Basically this big run up
we saw across the ecosystem.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.860
But when you think about
what actually happened,

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:05.700
the way that the cards
fell, how obvious was it?

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.793
Did it happen in the way
that maybe had been expected?

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:10.980
Yes and no.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:15.210
I think there were always,
as early as 2018, 2019

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:18.210
when the derivative
side of Bitcoin markets

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:20.790
and crypto markets more broadly
